  codingFactTwo:"The tool can reduce the time spent searching for syntax or writing boilerplate, leaving more time for design and testing.",
  codingFactThree:"Human review remains essential because a plausible suggestion may be incorrect, insecure or poorly adapted to the project.",
  takeawayLabel:"My analysis",
  codingTakeaway:"I see the assistant as a pair programmer, not an autopilot. It is useful when I can explain the generated code and validate it with tests, documentation and a review.",
  agentsTag:"Automation · Agentic systems",
  agentsQuestion:"What changes when an AI can act, not only answer?",
  agentsAnswer:"An AI agent can plan several steps and use tools such as files, APIs or a terminal. This makes automation more powerful, but a wrong instruction can also produce real actions rather than a simple incorrect sentence.",
  agentObserve:"Observe", agentReason:"Plan", agentAct:"Act", agentCheck:"Verify",
  agentsTakeaway:"An agent should receive only the permissions required for its task. Sensitive or irreversible actions need limits, logs and human approval before execution.",
  securityTag:"Cybersecurity · Data protection",
  securityQuestion:"How should a generative-AI application be secured?",
  securityAnswer:"OWASP and NIST recommend treating AI as a new application component with specific risks. Traditional controls still matter, but inputs, model outputs, connected tools and sensitive data also need dedicated protection.",
  riskPrompt:"Prompt injection", riskPromptText:"Malicious content attempts to override instructions or influence the model's behaviour.",
  riskData:"Sensitive data", riskDataText:"Prompts, logs or model responses may expose personal, confidential or proprietary information.",
  riskOutput:"Unsafe output", riskOutputText:"Generated content must be validated before it reaches a database, browser, command line or user.",
  securityTakeaway:"My minimum checklist is: avoid confidential data in prompts, validate inputs and outputs, restrict permissions, keep logs and test failure scenarios.",
  lawTag:"Europe · Responsible AI",
  lawQuestion:"What does the European AI Act change for digital projects?",
  lawAnswer:"The Act follows a risk-based approach. Since 2 August 2026, transparency rules apply to certain AI systems and generated content, while other obligations follow their own application timetable.",
  lawChatbots:"Inform the user", lawChatbotsText:"People must be told in relevant cases that they are interacting with an AI system rather than a human.",
  lawContent:"Identify synthetic content", lawContentText:"Certain generated or manipulated content, including deepfakes, must be labelled or technically identifiable.",
  lawLiteracy:"Build AI literacy", lawLiteracyText:"Teams need enough knowledge to use, supervise and question AI systems responsibly.",
  videoTitle:"The EU’s AI Act explained", videoSource:"Watch the video · 8 min",
  videoCaption:"Supplementary explainer by EU Made Simple. The official European Commission pages linked below remain the reference sources.",
  lawTakeaway:"For a developer, compliance starts in the interface and architecture: clear information for users, traceability, documentation and human oversight should be designed from the start.",
